  faqs: [
    {
      question: 'Which ERP systems can you integrate with?',
      answer: "We work through each ERP's supported integration path: the Service Layer and DI API for SAP Business One, the REST and contract-based APIs for MYOB Acumatica, SuiteTalk and RESTlets for NetSuite, and the published APIs for Microsoft Dynamics 365 Business Central and Finance and Operations. Older on-premises ERPs without a usable API can often be integrated through their database views, scheduled exports or an existing middleware layer. We confirm the exact route, licence requirements and any partner involvement during discovery, before quoting a fixed price.",
    },
    {
      question: 'Should we replace our ERP or build around it?',
      answer: "Usually build around it. An ERP is expensive and disruptive to replace, and most of the pain manufacturers describe comes from the gaps between the ERP and everything else, not the ERP itself. We keep the ERP as the system of record for orders, stock and costing and build the missing pieces beside it: shop-floor capture, quality workflows, traceability and reporting. If your ERP genuinely can't support where the business is going, we'll say so and help you scope a replacement with an ERP partner rather than rebuild it ourselves.",
    },
    {
      question: 'Can AI read our supplier certificates and delivery dockets?',
      answer: "Yes, this is one of the most practical uses of AI in manufacturing. A document processing pipeline reads incoming PDFs and scans, extracts fields such as supplier, PO number, batch or heat number, quantities and test results, checks them against the purchase order and specification in your ERP, and files the document against the right receipt. Each extracted field carries a confidence score, so staff review only the uncertain values and anything that fails a specification check, rather than retyping every document.",
    },
    {
      question: 'Do you build predictive maintenance systems?',
      answer: "We build the data foundation that predictive maintenance depends on: collecting machine and sensor data, linking it to assets, jobs and maintenance history, and making it queryable. On top of that we can build condition alerts and anomaly detection. Whether prediction is worth it depends on having enough history, including failures, for the machines that matter. We assess that honestly in discovery, and often recommend starting with condition monitoring and better maintenance records before any prediction model.",
    },
    {
      question: 'We are a mid-sized Australian manufacturer with a small IT team. Is custom software realistic for us?',
      answer: "Often, if it is scoped tightly. We start with a paid discovery to find the one or two workflows where connecting systems or removing re-keying saves the most time, then build those at a fixed price. All code goes into your own repository, the system runs in Australian cloud regions or on your own infrastructure, and we document it so your team or another provider can support it. Some manufacturing R&D may be eligible for the R&D Tax Incentive; speak to a registered R&D tax agent, as we are not one.",
    },
    {
      question: 'Can the system work on the factory floor with patchy Wi-Fi and shared devices?',
      answer: "Yes, if it is designed for it from the start. Shop-floor apps should work offline and sync when the connection returns, support shared tablets with quick badge or PIN sign-in, use large touch targets that work with gloves, and scan barcodes or QR codes rather than relying on typing. We test on the actual devices and in the actual environment before rollout.",
    },
  ],

